WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:01.430
Hey Thoughty2 here.

00:00:01.430 --> 00:00:04.020
Have you ever seen a flying bear?

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:05.020
No?

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:07.420
That’s because they don’t exist!

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:12.670
But fast forward 100 years and flying bears
could be freakin’ everywhere, okay maybe

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:14.070
not flying bears.

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:22.380
But something equally as amazing and terrifying
is happening across the entire animal kingdom.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:26.189
Creatures far and wide are evolving faster
than ever before.

00:00:26.189 --> 00:00:31.250
And the ways in which they’re evolving is
unlike anything we’ve ever seen.

00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:36.070
Through our vast studies into evolution we’ve
realised that it takes roughly one million

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:40.690
years to notice a significant change in an
animal’s characteristics.

00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:44.270
A major leap in its evolutionary life.

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:49.839
As we all know Zoologists, Bioscientists and
other experts have been constantly monitoring

00:00:49.839 --> 00:00:53.100
animals all over the world for hundreds of
years.

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:59.820
However, in the past 50 years a huge change
in how animals evolve has been observed.

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:04.840
Instead of a million years some animals have
made huge evolutionary jumps in just a few

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:10.210
decades and in some cases as little as 20
years!

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:16.330
In 1989 the international community passed
a ban on the sale of ivory worldwide, in an

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:22.730
attempt to stop the poaching of elephants
for their extremely valuable ivory tusks.

00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:27.000
Elephant poaching dropped significantly in
the years that followed and elephant numbers

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.340
in the wild started to rise again.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:35.700
But despite its illegality, the demand for
ivory never went away, especially in China,

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.190
where’s it’s highly coveted.

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:45.280
The two tusks from a single elephant can sell
for up to $400,000 on the black market and

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:51.780
so in this highly lucrative market criminal
gangs have met the ever-present demand for

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:54.920
the illegal collector’s item.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:02:02.480
Poaching levels have risen once again and
sadly, today around 35,000 elephants are slaughtered

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:04.760
every year for their ivory.

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:08.690
That’s over 5% of the global elephant population.

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:14.830
In fact, the number of elephants poached every
year is now far greater than before the ban

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:16.100
in 1989.

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:21.840
But the elephants are fighting back, yet they
don’t even know how.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:28.200
Every year more and more elephants are born
without any tusks.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:33.700
Because today an elephant is more likely to
survive and reproduce if it has no valuable

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:38.700
tusks for poachers to kill it over, even if
this means it can’t defend itself against

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:40.650
rival elephants.

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:47.910
In Zambia the amount of tusk-less female elephants
rose from 10 percent to 40 percent in just

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:50.140
20 years.

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:56.630
That’s evolution happening before our eyes
in an astonishingly short 20 years, and it’s

00:02:56.630 --> 00:02:59.990
all because of the actions of a few unsavoury
humans.

00:02:59.990 --> 00:03:07.540
In some areas of South Africa 98 percent of
female elephants have no ivory.

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:13.610
Most humans like living in cities, goodness
knows why, all the research shows that we’re

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:17.569
considerably less happy and less safe in cities.

00:03:17.569 --> 00:03:24.100
But nonetheless we have a natural desire to
swarm around giant metal hubs of weird modern

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:27.370
art and 24-hour fast food restaurants.

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:31.720
And our cities are growing, at a rate faster
than ever before.

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:38.160
Currently 54% of the global population live
in urban centers.

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:43.240
By 2050 It’s estimated to rise to 70%.

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:48.410
This will of course mean a far greater percentage
of the Earth’s natural habitats will be

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:56.330
destroyed so we can build more Mc-Kentucy-Fried-Subways
with vast splattering’s of Vegan Cafes,

00:03:56.330 --> 00:03:59.530
to support the growing urban population.

00:03:59.530 --> 00:04:06.160
This is of course bad news for avocados; avocado
abuse is already rising at a phenomenal rate.

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:13.110
But it’s even worse news for the animal
kingdom, or maybe it isn’t.

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:18.109
Destruction of natural habitats is of course
devastating to the species that once called

00:04:18.109 --> 00:04:19.349
that place home.

00:04:19.349 --> 00:04:26.289
But like the great African elephants, animals
far and wide are supercharging their evolution

00:04:26.289 --> 00:04:29.189
to adapt to life inside the city.

00:04:29.189 --> 00:04:35.629
Human’s are forcing them to, because it’s
either adapt to the urban environment or become

00:04:35.629 --> 00:04:37.319
extinct.

00:04:37.319 --> 00:04:42.159
Coyotes are traditionally found inhabiting
the great grasslands of middle America but

00:04:42.159 --> 00:04:48.130
in the past few decades they have spread across
the whole country and now inhabit every major

00:04:48.130 --> 00:04:54.270
city across America, where they have found
themselves a unique home.

00:04:54.270 --> 00:05:02.180
They are highly threated in their natural
habitat, the US government kills 400,000 coyotes

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:10.229
every year, under the guise of “Wildlife
Services” they call it “population management”.

00:05:10.229 --> 00:05:17.220
So heavily persecuted, coyotes have been forced
to masterfully adapt to the urban lifestyle.

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:23.960
They will eat quite literally anything, from
litter to bits of clothing found lying around.

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:31.139
Amazingly, after being monitored extensively
on CCTV it was observed that coyotes monitor

00:05:31.139 --> 00:05:37.409
traffic patterns and vehicle speeds in order
to cross roads safely.

00:05:37.409 --> 00:05:43.789
In Chicago alone there are an estimated 2,000
coyotes successfully living within the urban

00:05:43.789 --> 00:05:45.319
sprawl.

00:05:45.319 --> 00:05:48.599
Urban birds are also adapting their street
smarts.

00:05:48.599 --> 00:05:54.219
Scientists set up problem-solving tests for
different species of birds living in cities

00:05:54.219 --> 00:05:57.159
and then carried out the same test on rural
birds.

00:05:57.159 --> 00:06:02.650
The city-slickers were found to be significantly
smarter than their rural counterparts and

00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:05.729
were able to solve the problems far quicker.

00:06:05.729 --> 00:06:11.669
Urban birds have learnt to steal sugar packets
from tables outside restaurants and cafes

00:06:11.669 --> 00:06:16.490
and even steal food from people’s plates
when they’re not looking.

00:06:16.490 --> 00:06:22.409
These may not be examples of true evolution,
where a physical mutation becomes the norm,

00:06:22.409 --> 00:06:29.180
but they do represent huge changes in the
way animals behave due to human intervention.

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:35.800
And this is exactly how evolution starts,
it won’t be long before urban-dwelling animals

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:42.039
do evolve physically to meet the new demands
and challenges that a city lifestyle presents

00:06:42.039 --> 00:06:43.089
them with.

00:06:43.089 --> 00:06:45.279
And some already have.

00:06:45.279 --> 00:06:49.930
Just look at what humans have done with their
best friend, the dog.

00:06:49.930 --> 00:06:51.440
Humans created dogs.

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:54.490
15,000 years ago there were no dogs.

00:06:54.490 --> 00:07:00.159
We formed an unlikely relationship with grey
wolves and over the next few thousand years

00:07:00.159 --> 00:07:05.409
as our relationship with wolves evolved, so
did they.

00:07:05.409 --> 00:07:10.300
These fierce predators at the top of their
respective food chain became more docile,

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:16.490
their bodies shrunk, and so did their teeth
and paws, their ears went all floppy, so us

00:07:16.490 --> 00:07:19.020
humans would have to scratch behind them more
often.

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:25.669
There are now hundreds of breeds of dogs around
the world, with vastly different characteristics

00:07:25.669 --> 00:07:27.349
and personalities.

00:07:27.349 --> 00:07:33.789
Yet they are all able to reproduce with one-another
because they all came from the same species.

00:07:33.789 --> 00:07:40.389
Now if human intervention is able to create
an entirely new species of animal within such

00:07:40.389 --> 00:07:43.520
a tiny period of time, 15,000 years.

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:49.929
Then the possibilities are endless for how
other animals can and will change over the

00:07:49.929 --> 00:07:52.929
next 1,000 years and beyond.

00:07:52.929 --> 00:07:57.590
The common theme here is that we aren’t
trying to change animals, we didn’t intend

00:07:57.590 --> 00:08:03.539
to alter the wolf so radically, but through
rapid evolution, they have adapted themselves

00:08:03.539 --> 00:08:06.089
to fit around us humans.

00:08:06.089 --> 00:08:11.880
The exact same process is now happening on
an unimaginably large scale worldwide, due

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:12.880
to urbanisation.

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:19.780
The effects of human driven evolution in animals
is not just a theory.

00:08:19.780 --> 00:08:23.439
Like with the African elephants and their
ivory, the effects have been observed over

00:08:23.439 --> 00:08:27.099
countless species of animals in recent decades.

00:08:27.099 --> 00:08:32.240
Songbirds have changed their tune so their
acoustics are not lost within the constant

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:34.899
noise of the city below.

00:08:34.899 --> 00:08:39.709
Spiders in cities are getting far bigger than
their rural cousins, calm down, I’m not

00:08:39.709 --> 00:08:41.719
going to show an image.

00:08:41.719 --> 00:08:46.759
Insects, rats and mice are becoming immune
to insecticides and rat poisons.

00:08:46.759 --> 00:08:53.540
And bad news for those who are squeamish about
bugs, because bed bugs are fast becoming highly

00:08:53.540 --> 00:08:58.529
resistant to all the harsh chemicals we’ve
been throwing at them to try and exterminate

00:08:58.529 --> 00:08:59.529
them.

00:08:59.529 --> 00:09:06.790
In the past few decades bed bugs have become
250 times more resistant to pesticides and

00:09:06.790 --> 00:09:10.399
have developed significantly harder shells.

00:09:10.399 --> 00:09:15.870
Even weeds are getting in on the action, they’ve
developed a resistance to weed-killers, meaning

00:09:15.870 --> 00:09:22.100
we’re now going to have to rename all weed-killers
to weed-not-killers.

00:09:22.100 --> 00:09:26.959
Flowers that are often picked by romantic
park dwelling couples are becoming shorter

00:09:26.959 --> 00:09:33.000
and shorter every year, so they can hide amongst
the other plants and are therefore less likely

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:36.050
to get spotted and plucked from the ground.

00:09:36.050 --> 00:09:42.430
On a far larger scale pretty much all species
of animal across the planet have changed their

00:09:42.430 --> 00:09:46.490
fertility cycles due to global warming.

00:09:46.490 --> 00:09:51.520
Many species now breed earlier on in the year
than they used to, to account for the messed

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:54.000
up seasons brought on by global warming.

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:59.130
Another of the biggest problems that our urban
lifestyles are causing animals is chemical

00:09:59.130 --> 00:10:00.280
pollution.

00:10:00.280 --> 00:10:05.230
We use a lot of packaging and buy a lot of
shiny new technology, all of this requires

00:10:05.230 --> 00:10:11.870
the use of chemicals to produce, a lot of
these harmful chemicals will inevitably find

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:14.350
their way into rivers.

00:10:14.350 --> 00:10:19.850
Some fish are unable to adapt to this pollution
and are wiped out.

00:10:19.850 --> 00:10:26.250
Other species however have rapidly evolved
to become entirely resistant to toxins within

00:10:26.250 --> 00:10:27.629
the water.

00:10:27.629 --> 00:10:33.120
Atlantic killifish found in Passaic River
and Newark Bay in New Jersey are now able

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:39.100
to tolerate toxicity levels 8,000 times higher
than other fish.

00:10:39.100 --> 00:10:45.190
A trait they’ve been forced to develop due
to a nearby chemical factory leaking its toxic

00:10:45.190 --> 00:10:47.380
leftovers into the river.

00:10:47.380 --> 00:10:53.810
They can literally swim around in horrifically
toxic water all day long without any ill effects

00:10:53.810 --> 00:10:55.800
whatsoever.

00:10:55.800 --> 00:11:01.360
The amazing thing is secluded populations
of the killifish developed this anti-toxic

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:09.190
adaptation at the exact same time, without
coming into contact with each other.

00:11:09.190 --> 00:11:11.560
Humans may not be the most considerate of
species.

00:11:11.560 --> 00:11:16.320
For a long time, we have disregarded the needs
of our animal cousins in exchange for rapid

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:19.139
industrialisation and urbanisation.

00:11:19.139 --> 00:11:25.459
So it’s rather endearing to know that nature
will mould itself to fit around our foibles.

00:11:25.459 --> 00:11:31.029
And thankfully nature is powerful and smart
enough to be able to change so rapidly to

00:11:31.029 --> 00:11:36.769
a new, unhospitable environment, for if it
wasn’t many of the incredible creatures

00:11:36.769 --> 00:11:41.720
that we’ve come to care about would probably
be extinct today.

00:11:41.720 --> 00:11:44.420
Thanks for watching.

